// Title: Suspicious New Instance Of An Office COM Object
// Sigma ID: 9bdaf1e9-fdef-443b-8081-4341b74a7e28
// Status: test | Level: medium
// Author: Nasreddine Bencherchali (Nextron Systems)
// Date: 2022-10-13 | Modified: 2023-12-19
// ATT&CK: execution, stealth
// Tags: detection.threat-hunting
// Description: Detects an svchost process spawning an instance of an office application. This happens when the
//   initial word application creates an instance of one of the Office COM objects such as
//   'Word.Application', 'Excel.Application', etc. This can be used by malicious actors to create
//   malicious Office documents with macros on the fly. (See vba2clr project in the references)
// False positives:
//   - Legitimate usage of office automation via scripting
// References:
//   - https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/office/troubleshoot/office-developer/automate-word-create-file-using-visual-basic
//   - https://github.com/med0x2e/vba2clr
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/adf5a626c4a776f3f023056f0f398b9523a2f09e/rules-threat-hunting/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_office_svchost_parent.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where InitiatingProcessFolderPath endswith "\\svchost.exe" and (FolderPath endswith "\\eqnedt32.exe" or FolderPath endswith "\\excel.exe" or FolderPath endswith "\\msaccess.exe" or FolderPath endswith "\\mspub.exe" or FolderPath endswith "\\powerpnt.exe" or FolderPath endswith "\\visio.exe" or FolderPath endswith "\\winword.exe")
